<Base title="Sources and methodology" description="How TheTravelFact chooses a station, a source, and a date, and what was left off.">
  <article class="shell page prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
    <h1>How a number gets on the page</h1>
    <p>The first twenty sheets were checked 14 November 2026. Milan through Japan, and then Tokyo through Croatia, were checked 15 November 2026. Dubrovnik through Chile were checked the same day. A fact is a sentence plus a URL plus a date. The date is the day we read the source, not the day the climate period ended.</p>
    <h2>URL scheme</h2>
    <p>One place, one URL. A country is <code>/mexico/</code>. A city is <code>/mexico/cancun/</code>. The same pattern is used for Canada, the United Kingdom, Italy, France, the Dominican Republic, Spain, Germany, Japan, Ireland, Portugal, the Netherlands, Greece, Iceland, Switzerland, Austria, Czechia, Croatia, Hungary, Poland, Turkey, Morocco, Egypt, Costa Rica, Jamaica, the Bahamas, Aruba, Panama, Colombia, Peru, Brazil, Argentina, and Chile. Months stay in the table. They do not get twelve extra URLs.</p>
    <h2>Climate</h2>
    <p>Mexico uses Servicio Meteorológico Nacional normals for 1991–2020, issued 6 November 2026, one station file per place. Canada uses Environment and Climate Change Canada's 1991–2020 composite normals. The United Kingdom uses Met Office 1991–2020 location averages, except that the UK sheet is the HadUK-Grid national block printed on that page, not Heathrow. Italy, France, Spain, Germany, the Dominican Republic, Japan, Ireland, the Netherlands, Iceland, Switzerland, Austria, Czechia, and Croatia use the WMO 1991–2020 normals distributed by NOAA NCEI, one named station per sheet. Kyoto is not in that Japanese submission, so its sheet uses the Japan Meteorological Agency's own 1991–2020 normal. Portugal is not in the NCEI collection. Its sheet uses IPMA's 1991–2020 fiche and says the fiche calls the series hybrid. Greece is not in the collection either. The Athens image that was found was not transcribed, so Greece, Athens, and Santorini have blank climate cells. Where a month is blank in a file, the cell is blank. Where a temperature row is tagged with another station's number, or matches another station exactly while the rest of the file does not, that row is left off.</p>
    <p>Dubrovnik, Hungary, Poland, Turkey, Morocco, Egypt, Costa Rica, Jamaica, the Bahamas, Colombia, Brazil, Argentina, and Chile use the same NCEI collection, one named station, except where that collection has no file. Dubrovnik is not in the Croatian submission, so its sheet uses the DHMZ climate-atlas block labelled 1971–2000 and says so. Aruba is not in the collection. Its sheet uses the Departamento Meteorológico Aruba summary for 1991–2020, which does not name a station. Panama is not in the collection. Its sheet uses the Tocumen table headed 1977–2010. Peru's folder has no Lima station, and no 1991–2020 station table was opened, so the Peru cells are blank.</p>
    <p>We do not name a best month. The page restates the highest high, the lowest low, and the heaviest and lightest water-column months, and leaves the choice to the reader.</p>
    <h2>The other facts</h2>
    <p>Plugs and voltage are the IEC world-plugs country lines. The 15 November lines for the countries from Ireland through Chile were read from the 16 August 2026 archive of that page, because a direct download did not return it. Driving is a statute or a government page, quoted in our own sentences. Greece and Iceland have no driving line, because the statute was not opened. Tipping is a tourism office or, in Mexico, the consumer-protection agency, and only when that page states a practice. Tap water is whatever CDC's traveler page actually says. Ireland's page is more specific than the others and is quoted. The rest of the new countries, like France, Spain, Germany, and Japan, do not get a yes or a no. Currency names and codes are ISO 4217, maintained by SIX. Time zone names are IANA's. Where zone1970.tab has folded a country's traditional name into another city's zone, the sheet prints the name in zone.tab and cites the link in the backward file. Entry requirements are a link, not a matrix.</p>
    <h2 id="gaps">Left off on purpose</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Toronto (City) rainfall is blank for April through October, so the sheet prints precipitation instead.</li>
      <li>Vancouver Harbour has no rainfall element. The sheet prints precipitation and does not borrow the airport.</li>
      <li>No tipping percentage for Toronto, Vancouver, Banff, or for Canada as a whole. Mexico's page says tipping is voluntary because that is what Profeco wrote, not a percent.</li>
      <li>Venice Tessera's March average high is blank in the WMO file.</li>
      <li>Roma Ciampino is missing summer precipitation, so Rome uses Fiumicino.</li>
      <li>Puerto Vallarta station 14116 has no 1991–2020 normal. The sheet uses El Cuale.</li>
      <li>Ringway, the old Manchester Airport station, closed in 2004. The sheet uses Woodford.</li>
      <li>Naples Capodichino leaves May and July temperatures, and July precipitation, blank.</li>
      <li>Punta Cana's maximum-temperature row is numbered as Santo Domingo, and its minimums match Santo Domingo's. Those temperatures are not printed. Precipitation is.</li>
      <li>Japan's IEC line lists both 50 hertz and 60 hertz and does not assign a city, so the sheet does not either.</li>
      <li>Kyoto is not in the NOAA Japan submission. Its numbers are the JMA normal, and the position is the OSCAR record.</li>
      <li>Portugal's IPMA fiche says the 1991–2020 series is hybrid, including WRF-modelled values. The numbers are printed with that caveat. Greece has no NCEI folder, and the Athens table image was not transcribed.</li>
      <li>Zürich/Fluntern's file has no number-of-years row. The monthly values are still the ones in the file.</li>
      <li>No driving statute was opened for Greece or Iceland, and no tourism-office tipping line was opened except for Japan and Portugal.</li>
      <li>Dubrovnik's average daily high and low are the 1971–2000 atlas, not a 1991–2020 normal. The 1961–1990 block in the same PDF was not averaged in.</li>
      <li>Aruba's 1991–2020 summary does not name a station. Panama's Tocumen table is 1977–2010. Peru has no printed month table.</li>
      <li>Cairo's file prints the position without a hemisphere letter. Ankara's December minimum is written “-.3” in the file.</li>
      <li>Morocco and Brazil list two voltages. Jamaica's IEC line is 50 hertz. The sheets do not pick a voltage or change the frequency.</li>
      <li>No tourism-office tipping line was opened for Morocco, Egypt, Panama, or Chile. Turkey's line is about an esnaf lokantası. Jamaica's is about an all-inclusive. Colombia's is about a guide.</li>
      <li>The full list, including a Tacubaya file that disagrees with an older WMO copy, is in the repository file GAPS.md.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
